{"name":"Red Potion","hex":"#dd143d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"},{"term":"potion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small portion or dose of a liquid which is medicinal, poisonous, or magical."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To drug (someone)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/potion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Potion","blocks":[{"text":"A potion is a liquid that contains \"medicine, poison, or something that is supposed to have magic powers.\" It derives from the Latin word potio which refers to a drink or the act of drinking. The term philtre is also used, often specifically to describe a love potion, a potion that is believed to induce feelings of love or attraction in the one who drinks it.\nThroughout history, there have been several types of potions for a range of purposes. Reasons for taking potions have included curing an illness, securing immortality, and trying to inspire love. These potions, while often ineffective or poisonous, occasionally had some degree of medicinal benefits depending on what they sought to fix and the type and amount of ingredients used. Common ingredients in historical potions included Spanish fly, nightshade plants, cannabis, and opium."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Filtre_d%27Amour.jpg/330px-Filtre_d%27Amour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potion","wikidata":"Q1249918"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Potions.png/500px-Potions.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Potions.png/500px-Potions.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A collection of vials labelled as potions","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APotions.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/A_quack_doctor_selling_remedies_from_his_caravan%3B_satirizing_Wellcome_V0011377.jpg/500px-A_quack_doctor_selling_remedies_from_his_caravan%3B_satirizing_Wellcome_V0011377.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/A_quack_doctor_selling_remedies_from_his_caravan%3B_satirizing_Wellcome_V0011377.jpg/1280px-A_quack_doctor_selling_remedies_from_his_caravan%3B_satirizing_Wellcome_V0011377.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/A_quack_doctor_selli